schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Verifiable credential (wallet-held attestation)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A digital credential issued into a user's wallet once a verification
    system confirms an attribute or requirement is met; the holder chooses
    when to present it and when to keep it hidden.
  source_traditions:
    - decentralized-identity / self-sovereign-identity movement
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Described in identity-verification.md's "A Credential Is Created" step
    as what a wallet holds after a zero-knowledge verification succeeds, and
    as the thing a person can "show when you need to prove something, and
    hide when you don't."
  adjacent_meanings:
    - zero-knowledge-proof-selective-disclosure
    - onchain-attestation
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      Holding a credential is not the same as broadcasting it — the article
      stresses user-controlled disclosure as the differentiator from
      regular-internet data collection.
  tensions:
    - >-
      Building a portable reputation across platforms (the article's stated
      benefit) vs. the risk that a credential becomes a new form of
      cross-platform tracking if not designed carefully.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    The article does not name a specific credential standard or issuer, so
    "verifiable credential" here reflects the article's general conceptual
    framing rather than a specific technical implementation.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/identity-verification.md
    transformation: synthesized
    authorship: ai-assisted
